Looking at the figure, the equilateral hyperbola is centered at the origin with its transverse axis on the x‑axis, so it has the form x² − y² = a². The marked points (±2, 1) lie on the curve, so a² = 2² − 1² = 3, giving the hyperbola x² − y² = 3.

Reflecting across the line y = x swaps the coordinates: a point (x, y) on the original curve corresponds to (y, x) on the reflected curve. Substituting, the reflected curve satisfies y² − x² = 3 (a hyperbola opening upward and downward with vertices (0, ±√3)).

\boxed{y^2 - x^2 = 3}
